 reading:{primary:[["ST II-II q.34 a.2 (hatred of God)",NA("3034.htm")],["ST II-II q.36 a.1 (envy)",NA("3036.htm")],["ST II-II q.40 a.1 (war)",NA("3040.htm")],["ST II-II q.43 a.1 (scandal)",NA("3043.htm")],["CCC 2302–2317 (peace; avoiding war)",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/_INDEX.HTM'],["Francis (2020) Fratelli Tutti, §§ 256–262",'https://www.vatican.va/content/francesco/en/encyclicals/documents/papa-francesco_20201003_enciclica-fratelli-tutti.html']],
  secondary:["Festinger, L. (1954) 'A theory of social comparison processes', Human Relations, 7(2), pp. 117–140.","Smith, R.H. and Kim, S.H. (2007) 'Comprehending envy', Psychological Bulletin, 133(1), pp. 46–64.","The Kairos Document (1985) Challenge to the Church: A Theological Comment on the Political Crisis in South Africa.","Reichberg, G.M. (2017) Thomas Aquinas on War and Peace. Cambridge: CUP."]},
 scripture:[["Isaiah 2:4, they shall turn their swords into ploughshares",null],["Matthew 18:6, he that shall scandalize one of these little ones",null],["Wisdom 2:24, by the envy of the devil, death came into the world",null],["Matthew 5:9, blessed are the peacemakers",null]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. Hatred of God; acedia",html:`<p>Hatred of God directly implies the will's aversion from God, while other sins turn from God only indirectly, by inordinate desire for a creature. So hatred of God is the gravest of sins (q.34 a.2). Hatred of neighbour is opposed to love of neighbour; we may hate the sin, never the person.</p>
<p><strong>Acedia</strong> (q.35), sorrow at the divine good in us, is opposed to the joy of charity. Its 'daughters' include despair, faintheartedness, sluggishness about the commandments, spite, malice, and wandering of mind after unlawful things.</p>
${RC("p13",`<p>P3 L2: distinguishing <strong>acedia</strong> from depression. Pastorally, the 'remedy' for acedia (perseverance, work, prayer, not fleeing the cell) can harm someone with untreated depression; discernment is needed.</p>`)}`,
  check:Q("Why is hatred of God the gravest sin?",[["It is the most common","No."],["It directly implies the will's aversion from God","Correct: q.34 a.2."],["It is hidden","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Envy",html:`<div class="quote">"…another's good may be reckoned as being one's own evil, in so far as it conduces to the lessening of one's own good name or excellence. It is in this way that envy grieves for another's good…"<span class="src">ST II-II q.36 a.1 · Dominican tr. (1920)</span></div>
<p>Envy differs from <strong>fear</strong> (sorrow at an enemy's prosperity because it may harm us) and from <strong>zeal/emulation</strong> (sorrow that we lack a good another has, which may be virtuous if it spurs us to good, q.36 a.2). We envy those near us, rivals in reputation, not those far above or below. Envy is a capital vice, giving rise to gossip, detraction, joy at another's misfortune (Schadenfreude), and hatred (q.36 a.4). \"By the envy of the devil, death came into the world\" (Wis 2:24).</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Psychology · social comparison</div><p>Festinger (1954) proposed that we evaluate ourselves by comparison with others, especially similar others. Envy research distinguishes 'benign' envy (motivating improvement, close to Thomas's emulation) from 'malicious' envy (wishing the other to lose the good) (Smith and Kim, 2007). Social media, by multiplying comparisons with near peers, may intensify both; Thomas's insight that we envy those near us fits.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Which is NOT envy, for Thomas?",[["Sorrow at a rival's good as lessening one's own excellence","That is envy."],["Sorrow that one lacks a good another has, spurring one to good","Correct: zeal/emulation."],["Joy at another's misfortune","A daughter of envy."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Sins against peace: discord, contention, schism, sedition",html:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Sin</th><th>Opposed to</th><th>Thomas</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Discord</td><td>Concord of wills (in the heart)</td><td>q.37</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contention</td><td>Concord in speech (quarrelling)</td><td>q.38</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schism</td><td>Unity of the Church, the effect of charity</td><td>q.39: \"the schismatic intends to sever himself from that unity which is the effect of charity\"</td></tr>
<tr><td>War (unjust)</td><td>Peace between peoples</td><td>q.40</td></tr>
<tr><td>Strife</td><td>Peace between private persons (private warfare)</td><td>q.41</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sedition</td><td>Unity of a people (the city)</td><td>q.42: but resisting tyranny is not sedition</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Thomas holds that overthrowing a tyrannical government, which is directed to the ruler's private good rather than the common good, is not sedition, provided the disturbance does not cause greater harm than the tyranny (q.42 a.2 ad 3).</p>
${RC("t6l10",`<p>T6 L10: <strong>unjust law</strong> and apartheid. The Kairos Document (1985) argued that apartheid was a tyranny and that the Church could not remain neutral; Thomas's distinction between sedition and resistance to tyranny was part of that debate.</p>`)}`,
  check:Q("Is overthrowing a tyranny sedition, for Thomas?",[["Always","No."],["Not, provided greater harm is not caused","Correct: q.42 a.2 ad 3."],["Only if violent","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Just war",html:`<p>Thomas's three conditions (q.40 a.1):</p>
<ol><li><strong>Authority of the sovereign</strong>: not a private individual, who can seek redress from a superior.</li>
<li><strong>Just cause</strong>: those attacked deserve it on account of some fault.</li>
<li><strong>Right intention</strong>: the advancement of good or avoidance of evil; \"for it may happen that the war is declared by the legitimate authority, and for a just cause, and yet be rendered unlawful through a wicked intention.\"</li></ol>
<p><span class="voice">Magisterium</span>CCC 2309 lists strict conditions for legitimate defence: lasting, grave and certain damage by the aggressor; all other means shown impractical or ineffective; serious prospects of success; and no greater evils than the evil to be eliminated, especially given modern weapons. Francis, in <em>Fratelli Tutti</em> 258, judged it very difficult today to invoke the criteria of a 'just war', and urged the elimination of nuclear weapons (FT 262).</p>
<p>\"They shall turn their swords into ploughshares\" (Isa 2:4) remains the horizon.</p>`,
  check:Q("Which is NOT one of Thomas's three conditions for a just war?",[["Sovereign authority","It is."],["Just cause","It is."],["Right intention","It is."],["Economic gain","Correct."]],3)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. Scandal",html:`<div class="quote">"Scandal is, therefore, fittingly defined as 'something less rightly done or said, that occasions another's spiritual downfall.'"<span class="src">ST II-II q.43 a.1 · Dominican tr. (1920)</span></div>
<p><strong>Active scandal</strong>: the word or deed that occasions another's fall (the sin of the scandaliser). <strong>Passive scandal</strong>: the fall of the one scandalised. Scandal given by those in authority, or to the weak ('little ones'), is especially grave: \"he that shall scandalize one of these little ones that believe in me, it were better for him that a millstone should be hanged about his neck\" (Matt 18:6). <strong>Pharisaic scandal</strong>, taken out of malice from good acts, need not be avoided (q.43 a.7).</p>
<p>The clerical sexual abuse crisis is scandal in the gravest sense: crimes against children compounded by cover-ups that led many to abandon the faith. The Church's response must include justice for victims, safeguarding, and truth.</p>
${RC("p4l3",`<p>P4 L3: <strong>mandatory reporting</strong> and safeguarding. Charity toward the 'little ones' requires reporting abuse; concealment to avoid 'scandal' compounds scandal.</p>`)}`,
  check:Q("Scandal is…",[["any offence taken","No."],["something less rightly done or said that occasions another's spiritual downfall","Correct: q.43 a.1."],["a good act misunderstood","That is Pharisaic scandal taken."]],1)}
 ],
